#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166650 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1331279e-1a5f-5f6f-9104-0c88e1f9f6d0" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Slope of the Tangent to a Circle"}] :question/id #uuid "03bebdcd-5908-5c60-b35a-1c8f59593afd" :question/math-academy-id "e-9577" :question/problem "Find $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}$ for the circle shown above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3216/Source/Images/ed62e909ea502542ef307e57c0edb2a4.png)" :question/worked-solution "Since our circle is centered at $(0,-1)$ and has a radius of $2$, its equation is given by\n\n$$\n(x-0)^2 + (y - (-1))^2 = 2^2 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad x^2 + (y+1)^2 = 4\n$$\n\nNow, to calculate\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\n$$\n\nwe use implicit differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}(x^{2} + (y + 1)^{2}) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(4) \\\\\n\\frac{d}{dx}(x^{2}) + \\frac{d}{dx}((y + 1)^{2}) &= 0 \\\\\n2x + 2(y + 1)\\frac{dy}{dx} &= 0 \\\\\n2(y + 1)\\frac{dy}{dx} &= -2x \\\\\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= -\\frac{x}{y + 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166651 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1e118393-2388-5add-b516-e565abe1e36a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Slope of the Tangent to an Ellipse"}] :question/id #uuid "04c9673b-e4e5-508d-acc7-ea0888f2cf87" :question/math-academy-id "e-9578" :question/problem "Find $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}$ for the ellipse shown above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3216/Source/Images/fa26d004a0d7f5f86a31ea3f8071a534.png)" :question/worked-solution "Since our ellipse is centered at the origin and has semiaxes $2$ and $4$, its equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{2^2} + \\dfrac{y^2}{4^2} = 1 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad \\dfrac{x^2}{4} + \\dfrac{y^2}{16} = 1\n$$\n\nNow, to calculate\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\n$$\n\nwe use implicit differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{x^{2}}{4} + \\frac{y^{2}}{16}) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(1) \\\\\n\\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{x^{2}}{4}) + \\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{y^{2}}{16}) &= 0 \\\\\n\\frac{2x}{4} + \\frac{2y}{16} \\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx} &= 0 \\\\\n\\frac{x}{2} + \\frac{y}{8} \\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx} &= 0 \\\\\n\\frac{y}{8} \\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx} &= -\\frac{x}{2} \\\\\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= -\\frac{4x}{y}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166652 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "53a1649d-4279-5b7c-839c-728f333b2c3f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Slope of the Tangent to a Parabola"}] :question/id #uuid "58850b39-db80-532d-9f60-808a215da76c" :question/math-academy-id "e-9579" :question/problem "Find $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}$ for the right-opening parabola shown above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3216/Source/Images/6dfd6abbaf5d73a917c476962cd3cecf.png)" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the parabola has its vertex at $O$, and passes through the point $(3,-2)$.\n\nThe standard equation of a left- or right-opening parabola whose vertex is at $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\ny^2 = 4px\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x = 3$ and $y = -2$ into the equation above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(-2)^{2} &= 4p(3) \\\\\n4 &= 12p \\\\\np &= \\frac{1}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of our parabola is\n\n$$\ny^2 = \\dfrac{4}{3}x\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\n$$\n\nwe use implicit differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}(y^{2}) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{4}{3}x) \\\\\n2y\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{4}{3} \\\\\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{2}{3y}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
